1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:09,840
The cost of intelligence falls ten times a year. Demand rises faster.

2
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:13,375
So executives keep asking the same questions about where the money goes.

3
00:00:17,125 --> 00:00:22,910
Those answers shouldn’t live in a spreadsheet someone builds later.

4
00:00:22,910 --> 00:00:27,740
In Akka they sit right next to the system.

5
00:00:27,740 --> 00:00:31,155
Here are seven questions, in the order a finance leader asks them.

6
00:00:35,605 --> 00:00:48,280
The Overview starts with four figures: cost to date, the projected cost at period end against the owner’s budget, realized savings, and potential savings.

7
00:00:48,280 --> 00:00:50,875
Click one, and you are in the analysis behind it.

8
00:00:54,625 --> 00:01:02,130
Costs breaks the same money down by use case, model, team, app, or any tag.

9
00:01:02,130 --> 00:01:08,535
The whole setup lives in the address, so a finance lead can bookmark it.

10
00:01:08,535 --> 00:01:11,320
And usage without a price is footnoted, never hidden.

11
00:01:15,070 --> 00:01:20,525
Compare two periods and it reads like a financial statement.

12
00:01:20,525 --> 00:01:34,130
Cost rose by one hundred four dollars and seventy cents, and the bridge shows why: volume, model mix, prompt size, cache use, response size and price.

13
00:01:34,130 --> 00:01:38,305
The pieces add up to the cent.

14
00:01:38,305 --> 00:01:42,830
And one use case, ticket triage, nearly tripled after a model change.

15
00:01:47,280 --> 00:02:01,805
Savings finds avoidable cost: prompts that skip the cache, failed attempts, duplicate requests, reasoning above the median, unused tool definitions, and answers that run long.

16
00:02:01,805 --> 00:02:06,550
Each opportunity shows a saving range, its quality risk, and the evidence behind it.

17
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:16,415
Pick a model and the console prices the period’s real traffic on it.

18
00:02:16,415 --> 00:02:21,800
A new price list works the same way: you see the effect, model by model, before anything is published.

19
00:02:25,550 --> 00:02:31,575
For each use case, the frontier plots cost against pass rate.

20
00:02:31,575 --> 00:02:41,960
The owner sets the quality bar: a reference model, a judge, a tolerance, and hard gates that come from Verify.

21
00:02:41,960 --> 00:02:47,575
A cheaper model is only proposed when it clears the bar.

22
00:02:47,575 --> 00:02:54,560
Here the smaller model costs under a fifth as much and passes at ninety point two percent against ninety-one point six.

23
00:02:59,010 --> 00:03:04,805
A change is tried on recorded traffic first, then staged.

24
00:03:04,805 --> 00:03:13,380
The owner sets the stop rule in advance, so a failing rollout halts on its own, and nobody gets paged.

25
00:03:13,380 --> 00:03:15,885
Continue and Keep each record who decided, and why.

26
00:03:19,635 --> 00:03:25,500
The ledger keeps the estimate next to what was actually measured.

27
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:32,185
Four changes landed within their estimates. One fell short, and the ledger says so.

28
00:03:36,635 --> 00:03:45,810
Every budget has an owner, a plan to date, and a forecast with its range, and it names the day the money runs out.

29
00:03:45,810 --> 00:03:50,405
The owner sets the thresholds in advance: notify, reroute, or block.

30
00:03:54,155 --> 00:04:03,425
If you host a model yourself, the worksheet gives you an assigned price, utilization, and break-even.

31
00:04:03,425 --> 00:04:09,260
And Prices shows what is in force and what is not costed, so every figure traces back to a price.

32
00:04:13,010 --> 00:04:17,325
Cost is one part of one loop.

33
00:04:17,325 --> 00:04:23,260
A change is specified, built, verified, and run on the right model, with one evidence record behind it.

34
00:04:27,010 --> 00:04:29,565
Seven questions, answered where the work happens.
